  "enhanced_information": "Celiac disease is characterized by an immune response to gluten, leading to damage of the intestinal mucosa, which directly causes malabsorption of nutrients. This aligns with the consensus that Celiac disease alters the intestinal mucosa. In contrast, achalasia is primarily an esophageal motility disorder that does not affect the intestinal mucosa, and thus does not cause malabsorption through mucosal alteration. Cholestasis involves impaired bile flow leading to fat malabsorption but does not directly alter the intestinal mucosa. Crigler-Najjar syndrome is a disorder of bilirubin metabolism and is unrelated to the intestinal mucosa. Therefore, the only option that causes malabsorption through alteration of the intestinal mucosa is Celiac disease."
